  "text": "To find the sum of the \\( y \\)-coordinates of the intersection points between the parabola \\( x = 34y^2 \\) and the piecewise linear function \\( f(x) \\), we need to solve the equation \\( 34y^2 = f(x) \\) for \\( x \\) in the range \\([-1, 3)\\) and then find the corresponding \\( y \\)-coordinates.\n\nThe piecewise linear function is defined as:\n\\[ f(x) = \\begin{cases} x & \\text{if } x \\in [-1, 1) \\\\ 2 - x & \\text{if } x \\in [1, 3) \\end{cases} \\]\n\nWe need to consider the intersection points for \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\) and \\( x \\in [1, 3) \\) separately.\n\n### Intersection for \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\)\nFor \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\), we have \\( f(x) = x \\). So we need to solve:\n\\[ 34y^2 = x \\]\nSince \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\), we have \\( x \\in [-1, 1) \\), and thus \\( 34y^2 = x \\) implies \\( y^2 = \\frac{x}{34} \\). Therefore, \\( y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{x}{34}} \\). The \\( y \\)-coordinates are \\( y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{-1}{34}} \\) and \\( y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\). Since \\( x \\) ranges from \\(-1\\) to \\(1\\), we need to check which \\( y \\)-values are valid. The valid \\( y \\)-values are \\( y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\) because \\( \\frac{-1}{34} \\) is not a valid \\( y \\)-coordinate.\n\n### Intersection for \\( x \\in [1, 3) \\)\nFor \\( x \\in [1, 3) \\), we have \\( f(x) = 2 - x \\). So we need to solve:\n\\[ 34y^2 = 2 - x \\]\nSince \\( x \\in [1, 3) \\), we have \\( x \\in [1, 3) \\), and thus \\( 34y^2 = 2 - x \\) implies \\( y^2 = \\frac{2 - x}{34} \\). Therefore, \\( y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - x}{34}} \\). The \\( y \\)-coordinates are \\( y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - 1}{34}} = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\) and \\( y = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2 - 3}{34}} = \\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{-1}{34}} \\) which is not a valid \\( y \\)-coordinate.\n\n### Sum of the \\( y \\)-coordinates\nThe valid \\( y \\)-coordinates are \\( y = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\) and \\( y = -\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\). The sum of these \\( y \\)-coordinates is:\n\\[ \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} - \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} = 0 \\]\n\nHowever, we need to check the intersection points for the entire range \\([-1, 3)\\). The valid \\( y \\)-coordinates are \\( y = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\) and \\( y = -\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} \\). The sum of these \\( y \\)-coordinates is:\n\\[ \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}} + (-\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{34}}) = 0 \\]\n\nThe sum of the \\( y \\)-coordinates of the intersection points is:\n\\[ \\boxed{0} \\]",
